{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"L01\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"عَادًا and ثَمُودَا۟ appear as accusatives without a governing verb in this clause. The surrounding sequence supplies a judgment frame, but does not identify the omitted verb.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:37\",\"ar\":\"فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلرَّجْفَةُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"29:40\",\"ar\":\"فَكُلًّا أَخَذْنَا بِذَنۢبِهِۦ\"}],\"missing\":\"The syntax or a fuller passage would need to establish whether the names recover a verb such as “destroyed,” “remember,” or another governing verb; 29:40 groups the punishments but does not supply that verb locally.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L02\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The paired names show a formal difference in the base reading: عَادًا has tanwīn while ثَمُودَا۟ does not; the listed tanwīn variant for Thamūd levels that difference. A possible irony between the name ʿĀd and the root sense of return could color the repeated destruction theme, but the pairing itself does not establish that wordplay.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"missing\":\"A dictionary account establishing that the proper name ʿĀd derives from or carries the return sense, plus Quranic usage that activates that sense here, could decide the proposed irony. The supplied variant also needs a qirāʾāt source to establish its status independently.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L03\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The second وَ coordinates ثَمُودَا۟ with عَادًا as one paired mention; the conjunction binds the names but does not itself specify their shared fate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The two accusative names stand side by side before the clause about their dwellings.\",\"limits\":\"The shared judgment frame is supplied by the wider sequence, not by وَ alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"L04\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the base form, ثَمُودَا۟ has a different declensional surface from عَادًا; the listed tanwīn variant removes that asymmetry. The variation changes the formal hearing of the name pair, not its coordination.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The two names share the same accusative position but appear with different endings in the base form.\",\"limits\":\"The morphological contrast does not establish a semantic difference between the peoples.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L05\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The وَ before قَد turns from the paired names to a clause addressed directly to the audience: the dwellings make the past visible to them within the list of peoples.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The names are followed by قَدْ تَبَيَّنَ لَكُمْ, shifting attention from the peoples to what their dwellings disclose.\",\"limits\":\"The conjunction marks a transition; whether the clause is best parsed as parenthetical, circumstantial, or resumptive is not settled by وَ alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L06\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"قَدْ with the perfect تَّبَيَّنَ presents the clarity as already realized, strengthening the contrast between evidence available to the audience and the peoples’ misperception.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم\"}],\"activation\":\"The clause immediately names the audience and the dwellings from which the clarity comes.\",\"limits\":\"قَدْ establishes the completed clarity here; it does not by itself explain why the earlier peoples failed to heed it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L07\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ presents the matter as having become clear, without naming an explainer. That emerging clarity is framed against the closing مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ, and recalls the clear sign left for people who reason in 29:35.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:35\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَد تَّرَكْنَا مِنْهَآ ءَايَةًۢ بَيِّنَةًۭ لِّقَوْمٍۢ يَعْقِلُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The ayah opens its evidence clause with what has become clear to the listeners, then closes by assigning insight to the people who were diverted.\",\"limits\":\"The juxtaposition does not establish that تَبَيَّنَ and مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ are equivalent terms or that insight alone should have prevented their fate.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L08\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"لَكُم makes the addressed audience the recipients of the disclosure from the dwellings, placing them as witnesses to evidence that the named peoples did not follow.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The direct second-person plural contrasts with the third-person group whose deeds are beautified and who are turned away.\",\"limits\":\"The grammar addresses the audience with the evidence; it does not establish that every listener personally saw those dwellings.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L09\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"مِن links the clarity to the dwellings as its evidential source: what is clear to the audience comes from مَسَاكِنِهِمْ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The source phrase follows the verb of becoming clear and precedes the account of the peoples’ deeds.\",\"limits\":\"The source relation is clear in the base syntax, while finer distinctions such as source versus partitive nuance are not resolved by this phrase alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"مَسَاكِنِهِمْ makes former habitation the surviving channel of evidence. The preceding account ends with people lying in their dwelling, so the sequence moves from their fate in a home to clarity drawn from the dwellings of earlier peoples.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:37\",\"ar\":\"فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ فِى دَارِهِمْ جَٰثِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The dwelling vocabulary links the punishment scene in 29:37 to the remains that disclose the earlier peoples’ fate in 29:38.\",\"limits\":\"The text does not state that the dwellings are physically empty or that مَسَاكِن itself means ruin; their evidential role comes from the surrounding clause.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L11\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The وَ before زَيَّنَ resumes the account after the clause about visible evidence and shifts from what the audience can see to how the peoples were diverted.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ ۖ وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"A direct-address evidence clause is followed by a new verb with an explicit agent and affected group.\",\"limits\":\"This وَ links the clauses but does not itself encode a causal relation between the ruins and the beautification.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"زَيَّنَ casts diversion as the active beautifying of the people’s own أَعْمَالَهُمْ: the verb names the agent, the affected recipients, and the object whose appearance is worked on.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The following فَصَدَّهُمْ makes the beautification the immediate lead-in to turning them from the path.\",\"limits\":\"The noun أَعْمَالَهُمْ does not itself mean evil deeds; the danger described is their beautification and the resulting diversion.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L13\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"لَهُمُ marks the people as recipients of the beautification, while the outcome turns that apparently favorable “for them” relation into harm: they are diverted from the path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The recipient phrase is followed by a result in which the same people become the objects of صَدَّهُمْ.\",\"limits\":\"The lām marks the recipient; a beneficial meaning is not grammatically required, so the irony comes from the stated outcome.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L14\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The attached plural pronoun carries the named peoples across their dwellings, their deeds, and the obstruction that acts upon them, keeping the same group in view through the sequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated suffix and object pronoun link the opening name pair to each later stage of the account.\",\"limits\":\"This continuity clarifies reference but does not by itself add a separate cause for their destruction.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"L15\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The clause explicitly makes ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ the beautifying agent. A further wordplay reading could hear the proposed remoteness sense of شطن alongside the following turning-away عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The supplied root sense makes the association possible, but evidence that this derivation is active in Quranic use—such as a dictionary discussion and parallels linking the name شَيْطَان with distance language—would be needed to establish deliberate wordplay.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"أَعْمَالَهُمْ presents the deeds as the people’s own accumulated practice, while the sequence locates the danger in how those deeds are made attractive and then used to divert them.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The possessive suffix ties the deeds to the people, and فَ connects their beautification to their being turned from the path.\",\"limits\":\"The ayah does not identify which particular deeds are meant or say that every one of their deeds was evil.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The path-diversion in 29:38 can be heard alongside the Fatiha’s communal request for guidance: the prayer asks for the straight path and distance from those gone astray, while this ayah shows a people turned away from al-sabīl despite their insight.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha’s plural petition for guidance and its mention of those astray meet the focus ayah’s account of a plural people being turned away from the path.\",\"limits\":\"The Fatiha uses صِرَاط rather than سَبِيل, so this is a thematic path-and-guidance relation, not an exact lexical echo.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
